Anticonvulsant-Induced Severe Cutaneous Adverse Reaction

1. Patient Details

Patient Initials / Identifier: S.K.

Age: 34 years

Gender: Male

Weight: 70 kg

2. Suspected Adverse Reaction

Description of Reaction: High fever, mucosal erosion, skin detachment, and painful blistering covering 15% of body surface area (Stevens-Johnson Syndrome / TEN overlap).

Date of Reaction Onset: Day 21 post-medication initiation.

Severity: Severe / Life-threatening (required ICU admission and burn-unit care).

3. Suspected Medication(s)

Primary Suspect: Carbamazepine 200 mg orally, twice daily for focal seizures.

Date Started: Day 1

Date Stopped: Day 22

Action Taken: Carbamazepine stopped immediately upon symptom appearance.

4. Concomitant Medications

Medication: Levetiracetam 500 mg twice daily (continued).

5. Outcome & Action Taken

Action Taken: Immediate withdrawal of Carbamazepine; supportive fluid resuscitation and IVIG therapy administered.

De-challenge Outcome: Skin re-epithelialization began after 10 days of supportive care.

Re-challenge: Not performed (strictly contraindicated).

Patient Outcome: Recovered with minor skin scarring.
